From: freeman@MasPar.COM (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: MEDIA: Jurassic Park

    It is possible to do interesting science even with only a handful of
short fragments of DNA from an organism.  If I remember my classes correctly
(possible but not certain), a fair proportion of the functional DNA of most
cells codes for the RNA strands that make up ribosomes.  I seem to remember
that that proportion is on the order of ten percent, and by "functional" I
mean to exclude the large proportion of DNA that is repetitive short
sequences, perhaps just filler between the stuff that codes for things of
interest.
    Anyhow, it therefore follows that if you have even a smallish sample
of DNA fragments from an organism, there may be reasonable odds that some
of the fragments have to do with ribosomes, and if you can separate them
out you may be able to learn how ribosomes from that organism compare with
ribosomes from present-day organisms.

                       -- Jay Freeman (the other Jay; the other Freeman)

