Because I seriously doubt that melanin synthesis is that resource-intensive.
It's not like dark-complected people have more biomass tied up in their
skin --- in fact, if you'd ever lived with a black person you'd know that
blacks, on average, are far more sensitive to razor-burn than whites.  No
kidding!

> [* Yes I know there are varying theories, and combinations thereof, for
> explaining reduction in human body hair; I favour a combination of
> several, otherwise it is just too simplistic, Occam not withstanding.]

My favorite theory on this is the Montague Alexander one --- that proto-humans
went semiaquatic during the Pleistocene drought.  The loss of body hair is one
of several important ways in which we resemble sea mammals more than land
mammals --- others include the webs between our fingers (monkeys and apes
don't have 'em), the subcutaneous-fat layer half of us carry around, noses
adapted like a seal's for holding breath while underwater (other primates
simply can't do that), the fact that we mate face-to-face, the fact that
we communicate using complex sound more than physical motion, the fact that
babies can learn to swim before they can learn to walk, and a whole bunch of
other things I ain't got time to go into just now.
 
I think Scott DeLancey answered your points about dairying in another posting.

>              And one other factor I'd been thinking of several years ago
> (in connection with something else entirely) that may be relevant here:
