> in the world (besides height and a few other things).  More differing hair
> colours, eye colours, weights, etc (the record heaviest and lightest
> people were both "white", and in fact so were the tallest and shortest,

Hah.  You mean...they really *do* all look alike?  :-)

(Little post-colonialist joke there, gang.  Whoa...)

I could readily believe that Asians have less phenotypic variation than
whites.  Come to think of it, I think I've seen statistics on blood group
distribution that back that up.  I'm not so sure about blacks --- they're
an older and more varied population, if the cladists are right.

>                                                 One other factor in this
> variation bit may be that "white" is a bogus category, biologically. There
> are a LOT of differences between Celtic, Nordic, Slavic, etc. people, and
> the non-Indo-Europeans, like the Basque and Lappland Finns, are as
> different from say a German as Arab is from a Frenchman.  Still (to get to
> the point) it may just be that "white" humans are less stable genetically
> and mutate more often than Middle Easterners.  Yes this is WILD speculation.

Um.  The Lapland Finns aren't Europeans, they're Asiatics; so don't class them
as outliers.

Nevertheless, there's some evidence that you may be right about the high
plasticity.  Read Calvin William's "Ascent of Mind" for his glacial-pump
theory sometime.
 
> > Don't be surprised if it isn't; Europeans may well have been
> > dairying longer than the historical Middle Eastern cultures (Stonehenge is
> > older than the Pyramids, you know).
> 
