Sight and vision interests me very much.  I suffer from high-frequency
deafness (phones I always have to concentrate on) and have almost no
sense of smell (how do I smell? - terrible) and, I suspect, a diminished
sense of taste.  Seems to be inherited from my mother's side. 
Curiously, my mother, twin brother and I, all made the anosmia discovery
about the same time (a couple of months ago).  My hearing deficiency I
only found out about during a physics lesson, playing around with the
frequency generators.  So I value my sight (which, I am glad to say,
seems to superior to average) greatly.  Different sensory abilities to
the norm alter our lives.  For instance, my preferred mode of learning
is reading over all others.  Almost all of my vocabulary is learnt from
books.  On the music side, I come pretty close to having no tastes at
all.  Virtually all music leaves me stone cold, and the music lessons
taught at school might as well been encrypted, for all that I understood
them.  I was amused by Eric's post of his music catalogue - I recognised
not a single artist.

Anyway, back to vision and UV.  Yes many people can see into the UV
spectrum to varying degrees.  This is property of their corneas or
lenses, not their retinal cones.  As people age their corneas block out
more and more high frequencies, which is why old masters tend to over-
compensate by painting bluer pictures.  Perhaps the ultraviolet
sensitive children someone else mentioned, eventually grew up and lost
their UV sensitivity as they aged.  During the WW-II people who'd had
their natural lenses replaced with plastic ones, and consequently can
see UV (it stimulates the blue receptors), were used to spot otherwise
invisible ultraviolet sources.

Certainly an interesting story about the UV glasses, Harvey.

