Subject: SOC: Birth Order and Extropians

Quoth Robin Hanson, verily I saith unto thee:

> Frank Sulloway's much more thorough statistics said that "the overall
> probability that a firstborn will support a scientific revolution is
> 34%; the odds that a later-born will do so are almost double: 64%".
> 
> So either my stats are too low, I'm getting a biased sample,
> extropians violate the more general pattern, or there's a big
> difference between what people say and what they do here.

Or Sulloway's wrong >:)  Quite frankly I expected the opposite result. 
Particularly if the person was "very first born", i.e. an only child. 
Most of the only-children I have met tend to be (from my viewpoint) more
inquisitive, more open, and more flexible.  I think this stems from
children of multiple-child families tending to be forced into playing
roles, mainly through parental attitudes.  1st children are often given
more attention, and higher expectations are placed upon them.   

If Sulloway's right, my idea here, and your poll results, don't necessarily
contradict him.  I don't see you mentioning how he categorized
only-children. If he lumps them with 1st-borns, as I suspect, I think this
is a grave error.  I can say from experience that there is a HUGE
difference between only- and first-children.  When I was an only child,
all parental attention was mine.  My inquisitiveness and intellect were
constantly supported and rewarded.  When my 1/2 sister was born this all
changed.  As a 7 year old with a cute baby sister, I was of course
extremely jealous of the attention she received, and parents don't tend to
notice such things for quite a while.  My entire growing environment
altered radically.  I had to share, I had to be patient, I had to make
sacrifices, and I had to learn to adjust to no longer being The Only.  My
mother reported the same feelings when her brother was born (also when
she was 7).  

I realize that's anecdotal as all getout, but anyway, if I'm right on this
point, and only-kids do tend to be markedly more intellectual, I suspect
that brighter only-kids are less affected by suddenly becoming a
1st-born when a new sibling arrives [and despite the effects I report, I
suspect that I was far less affected than the typical "100 IQ" kiddo].  If
this is so, then the highly intelligent may well not lose their
curiousity, openmindedness, etc -- many of the things that make up
"intelligence" -- on becoming a 1st-born, thus the high openmindedness of the
1st-borns on this list.  I'd further wildly speculate that in AVERAGE
humans, this is all a moot point.  It is much easier to be reduced from
excellence to averageness that to be reduced from averageness to
terribleness, and with average people, some other dynamic may come into
play, thus yeilding Sulloway's results (I personally doubt that the
majority of the people he surveyed would be above average intelligence,
since the majority of people period are not.)  This "other dynamic" may be
something that is effectively made moot by the exploratory wills of
intelligent 1st-borners, and thus doesn't enter into it.  
Or something like that.  Criticize away...

> Btw, to deal with borderline cases, I'm just counting children born to
> both the same parents, and counting all the folks who say they just
> consider new theories more than others under "supporting" more.

If you did that in my case, I'd suggest that's erroneous.  I tried (not
very hard) to indicate that my 1/2 sister was, to me, my sister.  To
elucidate, HER father was my "father" from the time I was 2.5. I was not
even aware she was not my full sister until I was about 10, so there
would be no psychological differences between my relationship to her and
to a full-blood sibling. 

Also, I think another glitch in the poll is that few extropians would
admit to NOT being supportive of new theories, as that could be construed
by some as unExtropianly Correct (TM) neophobia.   It's a bit like
surveying a sunday school classroom to get data for a poll on whether
1st-borns are less likely to believe in god -- the results are skewed from
the start.  Neat effort though, and I think the subject is interesting.

